<idea-plugin>
    <id>dev.sasha.clauderemarks</id>
    <name>Claude Remarks</name>
    <vendor email="sasha.pchelintsev@gmail.com" url="https://github.com/p4elkin/claude-remarks">Aleksandr Pchelintcev</vendor>
    <!-- This element IS the Marketplace listing page. The README is never shown there, so anything a
         prospective user needs has to be here. Keep the first line saying Claude Code is required:
         without it the plugin is inert, and somebody finding that out after installing leaves a
         review that outlives the fix. The screenshot is an absolute raw.githubusercontent.com URL
         because the Marketplace cannot resolve a repository-relative path — the same shape the
         bundled stream-debugger plugin uses. -->
    <description><![CDATA[
        <p><b>Requires <a href="https://claude.com/claude-code">Claude Code</a>.</b> This plugin is a
        front end for it. With no Claude Code session to hand remarks to, there is nothing here to
        use.</p>

        <p>Attach short remarks to line ranges while you read code, then hand them all to a Claude
        Code session as one prompt: your note, the lines it points at, the code itself.
        <b>Remarks never touch your source files.</b> They live in IDE state only and stay out of
        version control.</p>

        <p>If you have used
        <a href="https://github.com/backnotprop/plannotator">plannotator</a> or
        <a href="https://github.com/umputun/revdiff">revdiff</a>, the shape is familiar: read a
        change, leave notes on the lines that need them, hand the notes to an agent. This one does it
        from inside the IDE, and three things follow from that.</p>

        <ul>
            <li><b>The review surface is your IDE</b>, not a browser page or a terminal overlay. Go
            to definition, find usages, the real diff viewer, the rendered markdown preview, your own
            keymap and theme.</li>
            <li><b>The review is continuous.</b> There is no session to open and none to close. Mark
            things as you read, publish when you have something worth sending, and a listening
            session picks up each batch as it lands.</li>
            <li><b>A question goes back to the session that already has the context</b>, so there is
            nothing to re-explain. The answer comes back into the IDE, onto the line you asked
            about.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The whole thing in four steps:</p>

        <ol>
            <li>Install the bundled Claude Code skill, with one button in
            <b>Settings | Tools | Claude Remarks</b>.</li>
            <li>Tell a session <code>listen for my remarks in this project</code>.</li>
            <li>Read, annotate, ask questions. Press <b>Publish Unread</b> when you have a batch
            worth sending.</li>
            <li>Profit.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>Remarks work in source editors over whole lines or a phrase inside one, in the rendered
        markdown preview, on the working-copy side of a diff, and with no file at all when the note
        is about the change as a whole.</p>

        <img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/p4elkin/claude-remarks/main/docs/images/remarks.png"
             alt="The rendered README with two annotated paragraphs tinted, the remark box open over it, and the Claude Remarks tool window on the right"
             width="750" height="409">

        <p><b>This is early software.</b> The path described above has been run by hand and works.
        Most of the rest has not been watched running, and it has had little use outside its own test
        suite. The two shell scripts the skill ships are macOS and Linux only, so on Windows the
        listening step needs WSL; everything else, including the clipboard route, works there.
        <a href="https://github.com/p4elkin/claude-remarks">The repository</a> says plainly what is
        proven and what is not.</p>
    ]]></description>
    <!-- The "What's new" panel on the Marketplace listing and in the IDE's plugin list. It covers the
         current version only, and nothing generates it: CHANGELOG.md is the full record and this is a
         hand-written summary of its newest section. Rewrite it in the same commit that bumps the
         version in build.gradle.kts, or the panel keeps describing the release before this one. -->
    <change-notes><![CDATA[
        <b>0.12.0</b>
        <ul>
            <li>The Claude Code skill ships inside the plugin. Nothing to copy by hand.</li>
            <li>Settings &rarr; Tools &rarr; Claude Remarks installs it, and says which version is on disk.</li>
            <li>A notification on project open offers the same install when the skill is missing or out of date.</li>
        </ul>
        <a href="https://github.com/p4elkin/claude-remarks/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md">Full changelog</a>
    ]]></change-notes>

    <depends>com.intellij.modules.platform</depends>
    <!-- Needed for ShowDiffAction, which opens the real diff for the files the `open` action names.
         A hard dependency, not optional: every JetBrains IDE ships VCS. -->
    <depends>com.intellij.modules.vcs</depends>
    <!-- The markdown preview: a remark written on the rendered page instead of on the source.
         Optional on purpose, and it has to be. The markdown plugin is bundled in every JetBrains
         IDE, but a person can disable it, and a hard dependency would then stop this whole plugin
         from loading — with no dialog and no visible error, the only symptom being that the tool
         window is not there. Everything that names a markdown class or group id lives in the config
         file, never here. -->
    <depends optional="true" config-file="claude-remarks-markdown.xml">org.intellij.plugins.markdown</depends>

    <extensions defaultExtensionNs="com.intellij">
        <!-- The id is part of the plugin's public interface: the platform derives the action id
             ActivateClaudeRemarksToolWindow from it, and the README documents that id for IdeaVim
             mappings. Renaming this breaks every user's .ideavimrc without any error. -->
        <toolWindow id="Claude Remarks"
                    anchor="right"
                    factoryClass="dev.sasha.clauderemarks.ui.RemarksToolWindowFactory"/>
        <intentionAction>
            <className>dev.sasha.clauderemarks.action.AddRemarkIntention</className>
            <category>Claude Remarks</category>
        </intentionAction>
        <intentionAction>
            <className>dev.sasha.clauderemarks.action.AskClaudeIntention</className>
            <category>Claude Remarks</category>
        </intentionAction>
        <postStartupActivity implementation="dev.sasha.clauderemarks.editor.RemarkGutterStartup"/>
        <httpRequestHandler implementation="dev.sasha.clauderemarks.review.ReviewRestService"/>
        <applicationConfigurable instance="dev.sasha.clauderemarks.settings.RemarkSettingsConfigurable"
                                 id="dev.sasha.clauderemarks.settings"
                                 displayName="Claude Remarks"
                                 parentId="tools"/>
        <notificationGroup id="Claude Remarks" displayType="BALLOON"/>
    </extensions>

    <actions>
        <action id="ClaudeRemarks.AddRemark"
                class="dev.sasha.clauderemarks.action.AddRemarkAction"
                text="Add Claude Remark"
                description="Attach a remark to the selected lines">
            <add-to-group group-id="EditorPopupMenu" anchor="last"/>
            <!-- A diff pane does NOT use EditorPopupMenu. The platform gives it its own group,
                 Diff.EditorPopupMenu, which ships empty. Without this line the item is missing
                 from the right-click menu in a diff, and the only way in is the shortcut. -->
            <add-to-group group-id="Diff.EditorPopupMenu" anchor="last"/>
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="$default" first-keystroke="control alt shift R"/>
        </action>
        <!-- The second entry point: a remark that asks for an answer, published on the spot.
             The stroke sits next to Add Claude Remark's so the pair reads as a pair. Checked
             against the IntelliJ Community checkout: no keymap that ships with the platform binds
             control alt shift A, in $default or in any of the Mac, GNOME, KDE and XWin keymaps.

             The id is a public interface the same way ClaudeRemarks.AddRemark and
             ClaudeRemarks.CopyAll are: README.md promises it will not be renamed, and
             ActionIdsTest pins it. -->
        <action id="ClaudeRemarks.AskClaude"
                class="dev.sasha.clauderemarks.action.AskClaudeAction"
                text="Ask Claude About These Lines"
                description="Ask Claude about the selected lines and get the answer back on the line">
            <add-to-group group-id="EditorPopupMenu" anchor="last"/>
            <!-- A diff pane has its own group, the same reason Add Claude Remark needs both. -->
            <add-to-group group-id="Diff.EditorPopupMenu" anchor="last"/>
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="$default" first-keystroke="control alt shift A"/>
        </action>
        <!-- The same publish as the tool window's toolbar button, so publishing does not need the
             tool window to be open. No default keystroke: this is a keymap entry the user assigns.

             The id keeps the word CopyAll even though the class and the text now say Publish.
             README.md documents this id as a public interface that will not be renamed, and
             ActionIdsTest pins it. Renaming ClaudeRemarks.CopyAll would break every .ideavimrc
             mapping to it silently: IdeaVim's ":action" fails on an unknown id inside IdeaVim,
             not here, so nothing in this project would ever notice. -->
        <action id="ClaudeRemarks.CopyAll"
                class="dev.sasha.clauderemarks.action.PublishUnreadRemarksAction"
                text="Publish Unread Claude Remarks"
                description="Render every remark that has not been read into one markdown prompt and put it on the clipboard">
            <add-to-group group-id="ToolsMenu" anchor="last"/>
        </action>
    </actions>
</idea-plugin>
